WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.116
This is Read Tabs, Play Guitar, by Fretbox. Guitar tab and notation, one step at a time.

00:00:07.916 --> 00:00:10.819
Today: strings and frets and reading tablature.

00:00:12.619 --> 00:00:13.591
Before you start.

00:00:14.391 --> 00:00:41.408
Millions of guitarists learned their favourite songs from tablature. Tab is simple: it shows you which string to play and which fret to press, and you can start reading it in five minutes. But tab is also easy to misread, and on its own it leaves out things you need, like rhythm. The ebook teaches you to read tab properly, to understand the standard notation that often appears above it, to find your way around the fretboard, and to practise with tabs in a way that actually makes you better.

00:00:42.908 --> 00:00:43.623
What you need.

00:00:44.423 --> 00:00:54.042
A guitar (acoustic or electric) in standard tuning, a tuner, and some patience with your fingertips: they will hurt a little for the first weeks, and then they will toughen up.

00:00:54.842 --> 00:01:06.835
If you have tab files in Guitar Pro or MusicXML format, a tab player lets you see and hear them at the same time, slow them down and loop the hard parts. The practice chapter explains how to use that.

00:01:08.335 --> 00:01:09.603
How to use the ebook.

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:20.912
Read one chapter at a time and do its practice section. The first chapters are about reading; the later ones are about playing: the fretboard, chords, scales and practice.

00:01:21.712 --> 00:01:27.919
The tab examples in the ebook use six lines, with the thinnest string at the top. Read them from left to right.

00:01:28.719 --> 00:01:39.152
Tip: Go slowly. Playing a passage correctly at half speed teaches your hands more than playing it badly at full speed. Speed comes from accuracy, not the other way round.

00:01:40.952 --> 00:01:44.211
Strings and frets. How the guitar is laid out.

00:01:45.011 --> 00:01:51.812
Before you read a single tab, you need to know how guitarists name the strings and frets, because tab is just a map of them.

00:01:53.312 --> 00:01:54.401
The six strings.

00:01:55.201 --> 00:01:58.103
Guitarists number the strings from the thinnest to the thickest:

00:01:58.903 --> 00:02:02.939
String 1: the thinnest, highest-sounding string, tuned to E.

00:02:03.739 --> 00:02:05.160
String 2: B.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:07.459
String 3: G.

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:09.757
String 4: D.

00:02:10.557 --> 00:02:12.179
String 5: A.

00:02:12.979 --> 00:02:17.629
String 6: the thickest, lowest-sounding string, also tuned to E.

00:02:18.429 --> 00:02:52.990
From the thickest to the thinnest, that is E-A-D-G-B-E. This is called standard tuning. In scientific pitch names, the strings are E2, A2, D3, G3, B3 and E4. With the A above middle C tuned to 440 Hz, the international standard, and equal temperament, the open strings sound at about 82.41, 110, 146.83, 196, 246.94 and 329.63 Hz, from the lowest to the highest.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:59.049
A popular phrase to remember the order from thick to thin: Eddie Ate Dynamite, Good Bye Eddie.

00:03:00.549 --> 00:03:01.962
Frets and semitones.

00:03:02.762 --> 00:03:14.955
The metal strips across the neck are frets. Pressing a string just behind a fret shortens the part that vibrates and raises the pitch. Each fret raises the note by one semitone, the smallest step in Western music.

00:03:15.755 --> 00:03:16.600
That means:

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.610
Fret 0 is the open string, played without pressing.

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:26.178
Fret 1 is one semitone higher, fret 2 two semitones, and so on.

00:03:26.978 --> 00:03:33.698
At fret 12, you reach the same note one octave higher than the open string. The pattern of notes then repeats.

00:03:35.198 --> 00:03:36.453
Hands and fingers.

00:03:37.253 --> 00:03:48.010
In guitar books, the fingers of the fretting hand are often numbered: 1 for the index, 2 for the middle, 3 for the ring and 4 for the little finger. The thumb is usually behind the neck.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:57.825
Tip: Press just behind the fret, not on top of it and not in the middle of the space. You will need less pressure, and the note will ring clearly instead of buzzing.

00:03:59.325 --> 00:04:00.046
Practice.

00:04:00.846 --> 00:04:04.341
First: tune your guitar with a tuner, string by string.

00:04:05.141 --> 00:04:13.700
Second: play each open string from 6 to 1 and say its name out loud: E, A, D, G, B, E.

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:20.820
Third: on string 1, play frets 0, 12 and 0 again. Listen to the octave.

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:28.418
Fourth: on string 6, play every fret from 0 to 12, one by one, listening to the semitones.

00:04:30.218 --> 00:04:33.477
Reading tablature. Six lines and some numbers.

00:04:34.277 --> 00:04:43.897
Tablature, or tab, is a picture of the guitar neck turned on its side. Each of the six lines is a string. Each number tells you which fret to press on that string.

00:04:45.397 --> 00:04:46.166
The lines.

00:04:46.966 --> 00:05:04.260
The top line is string 1, the thin high E. The bottom line is string 6, the thick low E. This catches many beginners out, because it is the opposite of how the strings look when you glance down at the guitar in your lap. Think of it this way: high strings at the top, low strings at the bottom.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:07.051
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:05:08.551 --> 00:05:09.285
The numbers.

00:05:10.085 --> 00:05:16.254
A number on a line means: play that string, at that fret. A 0 means play the string open.

00:05:17.054 --> 00:05:19.045
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:05:19.845 --> 00:05:28.125
Read it from left to right: string 2 open, then fret 1, then fret 3; then string 1 open, fret 1 and fret 3.

00:05:29.625 --> 00:05:30.518
Chords in tab.

00:05:31.318 --> 00:05:38.365
When numbers are stacked vertically, play those strings together. This is an open E minor chord, strummed all at once:

00:05:39.165 --> 00:05:41.156
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:05:42.656 --> 00:05:44.474
What simple tab does not tell you.

00:05:45.274 --> 00:05:59.001
Basic text tab shows what to play, but usually not how long each note lasts. The spacing between numbers is only a rough guide to the rhythm. That is why it helps to listen to the song while you read, or to use tab with rhythm notation.

00:05:59.801 --> 00:06:13.619
Tab made in programs such as Guitar Pro, or shared in formats such as MusicXML, usually includes the rhythm too, shown with note stems and beams under or over the tab, and a tab player can play it back so you can hear exactly how it goes.

00:06:15.119 --> 00:06:15.840
Practice.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:21.893
First: play the two tab examples above slowly, saying the string and fret out loud.

00:06:22.693 --> 00:06:27.845
Second: write a short tab of something you can already play, even a single melody line.

00:06:28.645 --> 00:06:34.545
Third: find a tab for a song you know well. Read it while listening to the recording and follow along.

00:06:36.345 --> 00:06:37.406
That's all for today.

00:06:38.206 --> 00:06:39.408
If you want to keep going:

00:06:40.208 --> 00:06:51.620
Fretbox is the app behind the ebook. It is a tab player for guitarists: it opens tablature files in the Guitar Pro and MusicXML formats, so you can read a song and hear it at the same time.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:55.057
Everything the ebook teaches is meant to be used with it:

00:06:55.857 --> 00:06:58.617
reading tablature and the symbols for each technique.

00:06:59.417 --> 00:07:02.016
following the rhythm in the notation that comes with the tab.

00:07:02.816 --> 00:07:05.547
learning the fretboard, chords and scales.

00:07:06.347 --> 00:07:10.661
practising song by song, section by section, at your own pace.

00:07:11.461 --> 00:07:15.606
This ebook is free. Share it with anyone who is starting out on the guitar.

00:07:16.406 --> 00:07:20.967
This episode of Read Tabs, Play Guitar was narrated with a synthetic voice.

